## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to integrate a simple QoS mechanism with a basic I/O scheduler, showing end-to-end flow from I/O request to wakeup. Demonstrates reasoning about blocking, concurrency, and fairness under bursts.

## Key Concepts

- Token bucket I/O throttling
- Per-process QoS guarantees
- Blocking, wakeup, and race avoidance
- Simple ready-queue scheduling and aging for fairness
- Testability with burst scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal sketch: token bucket I/O
typedef struct { int tokens; int capacity; } bucket;
bucket buckets[MAX_PROCS];

int io_request(int pid, int bytes){
  if (buckets[pid].tokens >= bytes){
    buckets[pid].tokens -= bytes;
    submit_disk(pid, bytes);
    return 1;
  } else {
    enqueue_io(pid, bytes);
    block(pid);
    return 0;
  }
}

void refill_all(int per_tick){
  for (int i = 0; i < MAX_PROCS; i++){
    buckets[i].tokens = min(buckets[i].tokens + per_tick, buckets[i].capacity);
    wakeup_if_waiting(i);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test fairness under bursty workloads?
- How would you handle dynamic budget changes per process?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of demand paging, stack growth, race conditions, and memory protection at OS level; evaluates handling of concurrency and TLB effects.

## Key Concepts
- Guard pages and demand paging
- Atomic CAS for lock-free growth
- Per-thread stack limits and fairness
- Page table updates and TLB flush semantics

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code: grow_stack(thread, fault_addr)
size_t new_base;
if (fault_addr < thread->stack_base - PAGE_SIZE) {
  if (atomic_cas(&thread->stack_base, fault_addr_base, fault_addr_base - PAGE_SIZE)) {
     page_t *p = allocate_page();
     map_page(thread->pid, new_base, p, PERM_READ_WRITE);
     flush_tlb(thread);
     return RESUME;
  } else {
     // another thread grew
     return retry_fault();
  }
}
```

