## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

## Why This Is Asked
Signals interrupting kernel I/O are a core but subtle area; beginners must reason about races and correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Blocking I/O, signals, race conditions, atomic flags, wakeups
- EINTR semantics and partial reads
- Memory visibility and CAS-based state updates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C implementation sketch for read_with_signal
int read_with_signal(int fd, void *buf, int n, int *interrupted){
  int total = 0;
  while (total < n){
    int got = kernel_read(fd, (char*)buf + total, n - total);
    if (got > 0){ total += got; continue; }
    if (pending_signal(fd_thread_id)){ *interrupted = 1; return -1; }
    // block until wake
  }
  return total;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multiple signals, and nesting of signals?
- How would you test race conditions between signal delivery and I/O completion?

